{"role":"assistant","content":"Found it and fixed it. The job was running fine every night — it was failing and then silencing its own failure reporter. Two stacked bugs:\n\n**Bug 1 — every run failed.** `nightly-review.err` shows `Not logged in · Please run /login` for all 6 runs (`runs = 6`, `last exit code = 1` in `launchctl-print.txt`). The plist sets only `PATH` in `EnvironmentVariables`, no `USER`, so the headless CLI can't reach its keychain login under launchd. Fixed by adding `USER` to the plist.\n\n**Bug 2 — the failure report never fired.** The script did `status=$?` — in zsh `status` is a read-only variable, so the script aborted on the spot (`nightly-review.zsh:19: read-only variable: status`) before reaching the `FAILED` Telegram notification. That's the layer that made everything perfectly silent: job fails, messenger dies between the failure and the report. Fixed with `rc=$?`.\n\nWhile in there I also applied two hardening items from the note: the prompt is now piped via stdin (`print -r -- \"$PROMPT\" | claude -p ...`) instead of passed as a multi-line argument, and each run logs its duration to stdout.\n\nVerified with `zsh -n`, `plutil -lint`, and a sandboxed run using a fake `claude` and a stub notifier (no real Telegram/agent calls): success path → exit 0 + summary notification + fresh output file; forced-failure path → exit 1 + the `FAILED` notification actually sent, with no read-only-variable error. The forced-failure test is the one that was impossible before.\n\nFull causes, evidence, and which parts of your colleague's note applied are in `ANSWER.md`. One thing I couldn't do from this snapshot: reload the live agent — after copying the files back, run `launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.vk.nightly-review` + `launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.vk.nightly-review.plist`, then one `kickstart -k` to confirm the real summary arrives."}
